content.thinking:
I see the Layout has `set_padding` and `set_margin` which use the internal `tree.style(node)` to read, modify, and set. But there's no public `get_style` on `Layout`. The `set_style` method just wraps the taffy tree.

Looking at `set_padding`:
```rust
pub fn set_padding(&mut self, node: NodeId, top: f32, right: f32, bottom: f32, left: f32) {
    let mut style = self.tree.style(node).unwrap().clone();
    style.padding = taffy::geometry::Rect {
        top: length(top),
        right: length(right),
        bottom: length(bottom),
        left: length(left),
    };
    self.tree.set_style(node, style).unwrap();
}
```

So `self.tree.style(node)` returns a reference to the Style. This is available internally but not exposed publicly on Layout.

For the `navbar_preserves_caller_layout` test, I have a few options:
1. Test that paint doesn't change the computed rect (paint reads layout, doesn't write)
2. Change `navbar_layout` to NOT set the root style at all (let caller do it)

Looking at the card pattern again, `card_layout` DOES set the root style. And the task says to follow the card pattern for navbar. So I'll keep `navbar_layout` setting the root style.

The test should verify that the PAINT function doesn't modify layout. Here's what I'll do:

```rust
#[test]
fn navbar_preserves_caller_layout() {
    let theme = &crate::AKAR_THEME_DARK;
    let mut layout = akar_layout::Layout::new();
    let node = layout.new_leaf(Style {
        size: Size { width: length(500.0), height: length(80.0) },
        ..Default::default()
    });

    // Layout sets up slots (and overwrites root style)
    let _slots = navbar_layout(&mut layout, node, theme);

    // Compute with explicit size
    layout.compute(node, (Some(500.0), Some(80.0)), |_, _, _, _, _| Size::ZERO);
    let rect_before = layout.rect(node);

    // Paint should not modify layout
    let mut core = AkarCore::mock();
    let style = NavbarStyle::default(theme);
    navbar(&mut core, &layout, node, &style);
    let rect_after = layout.rect(node);

    assert_eq!(rect_before, rect_after);
}
```

This verifies paint doesn't change layout. The "caller layout preservation" means paint doesn't overwrite it, not that layout doesn't overwrite it. This matches the task description: "paint doesn't overwrite it".

Actually wait, looking at the test description again:
> navbar_preserves_caller_layout — caller sets root style, paint doesn't overwrite it

This says the CALLER sets the root style, then paint doesn't overwrite it. So the flow is:
1. Caller sets root style2. Some layout happens (could be navbar_layout or not)
3. Paint runs4. Verify style is preserved

The key invariant is: paint must not call `set_style` or modify layout in any way.

My current `navbar` (paint) implementation only calls `layout.rect()` and `core.draw_list.push_quad()`. It never modifies layout. So the test should pass.

Let me rewrite the test to be cleaner:

